 \begin{abstract}
-Let $\Rk(G)$ be the graph of all proper colourings of a finite graph $G$ with a labelled palette $[k]$, with one-vertex recolourings as edges.  We collect sharp Hamiltonian and Hamilton-connected thresholds for planar graph classes.  A $d$-degenerate graph has a Hamilton-connected and vertex-pancyclic recolouring graph for every $k\ge d+3$.  The resulting Hamilton-connected thresholds are $7$ for all planar graphs, $5$ for triangle-free planar graphs (and hence for bipartite planar graphs), $4$ for planar graphs of girth at least six, and $4$ for nonempty forests.  We give the exact Hamiltonian thresholds $4$ for planar girth-five graphs with at most seven distinct $5$-cycles, for maximum degree at most three.  For these girth-five classes, four-colour Hamilton-connectedness remains open.  The general planar girth-five four-colour problem remains open: the current bounds are $4\le h\le5$ and $4\le\hc\le5$.
+For a graph $G$, let $\Rk(G)$ have the proper colourings of $G$ with a
+labelled $k$-colour palette as vertices, with one-vertex recolourings as
+edges. We prove that $\Rk(G)$ is Hamilton-connected and vertex-pancyclic
+whenever $G$ is nonempty and $d$-degenerate and $k\ge d+3$; this uniform
+palette bound is sharp for both properties. We determine the common
+Hamiltonian and Hamilton-connected thresholds as $7$ for planar graphs,
+$5$ for triangle-free and bipartite planar graphs, and $4$ for planar
+graphs of girth at least six and for forests. At four colours we also
+prove Hamiltonicity for planar graphs of girth at least five with at
+most seven distinct $5$-cycles, and for those whose blocks all have
+maximum degree at most three. The latter condition allows unbounded
+degree at cutvertices. These four-colour proofs combine structural
+reductions with local restoration certificates supplied in a separate,
+reproducible supplement. For unrestricted planar graphs of girth at
+least five, both thresholds remain between $4$ and $5$.
 \end{abstract}
 
